Comments:       WARNING not a normal TCE, do not have this in your boot list!
                An updated bios is better, but not all makers offer bios
                updates for older PCs. Before loading this TCE run 
                $ dmesg | grep micro # no need to type microcode.
                Microcodes affect only the x86 instruction set so please
                get an bios update to get more features.

                Please read all this info as its designed for both 32 & 64 bit users
                AND for MBR and UEFI users....so you need to understand your own
                pathways to files for below. So below....as long as you edit to the correct path 
                to your microcode file...it could go in
                /boot or /boot/grub or /EFI or /EFI/BOOT or /EFI/boot 
                That is, below are just examples. So if you want to place the file slightly 
                different to above....just match that pathway for your bootloader config.
                
                Load this TCE once and copy the original intel-ucode.img from
                /tmp/tcloop/intel-ucode/usr/local/share/intel-ucode/files Dir 
                to your boot dir with above caveats. Note copying sym links will not work.

                You may need root powers to do that copying?
                Then use local or root powers to edit your grub.cfg line for initrd
                so the beginning of the line may read
                initrd /boot/intel-ucode.img (subject to your above personal preferences)
                (for UEFI it may read ....initrd /EFI/BOOT/intel-ucode.img etc)                

                Full reboot and recheck if the above dmesg command  has changed....I can not 
                update my bios unless I use Microsoft so I am stuck with a out-of-date bios 
                so the microcodes help me YMMV. Image of result for me. YMMV
                https://i.postimg.cc/SRcwYyLc/micro.png
                Notice the key words...updated early from my old microcode value.
                I am researching the SRBDS issue that affects me hopefully not you.               
                      
